    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "[auto-routed: 代码实验走真实执行] Execute a numerical scan to test the Barron Space Hypothesis by training small feedforward networks on increasingly many analytic constraints (w) and measuring the minimum hidden layer width N_c(w) required to achieve zero violation (max error ≤ 1e-3) on held-out constraints. The plan includes: (1) generating synthetic constraint functions from Barron-space classes (e.g., smooth functions with bounded Fourier norms), (2) sweeping w and N to record training and leave-out errors, (3) extracting N_c(w) as the minimal width achieving zero violation, and (4) validating the predicted logarithmic scaling N_c(w) ∝ O(log w) against the empirical data. All outputs are finite, and the experiment respects the zero-violation threshold and small-network-only constraint."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "skill",
      "value": "None (custom ML workflow)"
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "expected_prediction",
      "value": "The plot of N_c(w) vs. log w will show approximately linear growth with slope consistent with Barron space capacity bounds (e.g., N_c(w) ≈ a log w + b for constants a, b), supporting the quasi-rigid hypothesis. N_c(w) will remain finite across the scanned w range, and leave-out errors will drop below 1e-3 at N_c(w) while training errors remain near zero."
    },
